by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-25T13:13:24Z

Thank you @HypeMC. I reworked this a bit and pushed the result.

`Worker::isStopping()` is not the right predicate. It returns `$shouldStop`, which is set by anything that calls `Worker::stop()`: `StopWorkerOnMessageLimitListener`, `OnTimeLimit`, `OnMemoryLimit`, `OnFailureLimit`, `OnCustomStopException`, `OnRestartSignal`, or a user listener. Once one of those has fired, the very *first* `SIGINT` force-quits, and the log says "Received signal 2 again" when no earlier signal existed:

```
messenger:consume --limit=1, a single kill -INT during post-stop work

[1.027] Stopping worker. / Worker stopped due to maximum count of 1 messages processed
[1.027] WorkerStoppedEvent listener START (no signal was sent yet)
[3.939] Received signal 2 again, forcing exit.   -> exit 130
```

On 8.2 the same run is graceful and exits 0. This is not a corner case: `messenger:failed:retry` registers `StopWorkerOnMessageLimitListener(1)` per message, so its worker is always in the stopping state after each retried message. And with batch handlers or `--concurrency`, `flush(true)` after a limit-triggered stop keeps messages in flight for as long as they take, which is exactly the window where one Ctrl+C would now kill them.

So the flag now tracks "a stop signal was received" on the command. `ConsumeMessagesCommand` gets a private `$shouldStop`, and `FailedMessagesRetryCommand` reuses the one it already had, which is written only in `handleSignal()`.

That also means `Worker::isStopping()` is no longer needed, so I removed it along with its changelog line. All 8 of your tests still pass unchanged, plus one regression test per command. `Worker.php` is untouched and the PR adds no public API. I reworded the remaining changelog entry, since "while the worker is already stopping" described the behaviour we just fixed.

Two things for the docs PR, both measured on real processes:

Force quitting leaves the in-flight message neither acked nor rejected. Doctrine, Redis and MongoDB default `redeliver_timeout` to 3600s, so it is parked for up to an hour and its partial side effects are duplicated on redelivery. Under `--concurrency=N` this applies to up to N messages at once, not one: with `--concurrency=3` and two handlers running in separate child processes, both were killed and neither was settled. No child process is orphaned, they die with the parent.

It cannot help a handler blocked inside a C call. PHP installs signal handlers with `SA_RESTART`, so with a handler blocked on a read both signals were ignored and the process needed `SIGKILL` after 25s. Worth one sentence so nobody expects otherwise.

One question I could not answer: with `--concurrency`, is there anything you would want done to the worker pool before the parent exits, or is letting the children die with it the intended behaviour?

