by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-02T15:07:54Z

Thank you @HypeMC.

Answering my own question: letting the children die is the intended behavior, and it turns out to be deterministic rather than incidental. amphp spawns pool workers with SIGINT ignored (only the parent ever sees Ctrl+C), and its destructors SIGKILL any still-running child when the parent exits, so nothing lingers, not even a child stuck in a blocking C call. Draining the pool instead would wait on the very handler being killed, and settling in-flight envelopes cannot be done soundly mid-handler: unsettled + `redeliver_timeout` is the transport contract for a crashed consumer, which a force quit is by request.
